# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious package, for scanner tests.
# Never run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: litellm-1.82.8
title: "litellm 1.82.8 (PyPI): .pth file runs a credential stealer at every interpreter start"
campaign: teampcp-trivy-2026
date: "2026-03-24"
kind: wheel
ecosystem: pip
name: litellm
version: "1.82.8"
benignVersion: "1.82.6"
summary: >-
  On 2026-03-24 the TeamPCP actor published litellm 1.82.7 and 1.82.8 to PyPI
  from the hijacked maintainer account (the project's own CI had run the
  poisoned Trivy v0.69.4). 1.82.7 hid the payload in proxy_server.py, run on
  import; 1.82.8 added litellm_init.pth, a one-line file at the site-packages
  root that Python's site module executes at every interpreter start — pip
  included — so the stealer ran even when litellm was never imported. It
  collected environment variables, SSH keys, cloud and Kubernetes credentials
  and .env files, encrypted them with a session key wrapped by an embedded RSA
  key, exfiltrated the bundle, and installed a ~/.config systemd user poller
  (sysmon.py) that fetched and ran further payloads from a C2. A bug — each
  child reprocessed the .pth and forked again — is what got it noticed. The
  Docker image was not affected.
techniques: [account-takeover, pth-startup-execution, persistence-systemd, credential-theft, c2-poller]
reconstruction: >-
  litellm_init.pth is the single import line a .pth runs at startup, launching
  _bootstrap.py detached. _bootstrap.py collects the environment and credential
  files, encrypts them with openssl (session key + RSA-OAEP wrap, embedded
  throwaway key), POSTs the bundle to a defanged host, and writes a systemd
  user unit plus sysmon.py — the 300s-then-3000s poller that downloads a URL to
  /tmp/pglog and runs it, skipping youtube.com, recording the last URL in
  /tmp/.pg_state — all matching Kudelski's and StepSecurity's description. Every
  endpoint (exfil host, C2) is defanged (".invalid"), and the payload is
  readable rather than the original's double-base64 blob. The rest of litellm is
  a small stand-in for the real library.
references:
  - https://docs.litellm.ai/blog/security-update-march-2026
  - https://labs.boostsecurity.io/articles/teampcp-litellm-supply-chain-compromise/
  - https://www.truesec.com/hub/blog/malicious-pypi-package-litellm-supply-chain-compromise
  - https://futuresearch.ai/blog/litellm-pypi-supply-chain-attack/
  - https://kudelskisecurity.com/research/investigating-two-variants-of-the-trivy-supply-chain-compromise
indicators:
  - litellm_init
  - sysmon.py
  - pglog
-- shared/litellm/__init__.py --
"""litellm: call 100+ LLM APIs using the OpenAI input/output format."""

__version__ = "1.82.8"

from litellm.main import completion, acompletion
from litellm.utils import get_llm_provider, token_counter

__all__ = ["completion", "acompletion", "get_llm_provider", "token_counter", "__version__"]
-- shared/litellm/main.py --
"""The completion entry points."""

from litellm.utils import get_llm_provider


def completion(model, messages, **kwargs):
    """Route a chat completion to the provider that owns `model`."""
    provider, model_name = get_llm_provider(model)
    return {"provider": provider, "model": model_name, "choices": []}


async def acompletion(model, messages, **kwargs):
    """Async variant of completion()."""
    return completion(model, messages, **kwargs)
-- shared/litellm/utils.py --
"""Small helpers shared across the package."""

_PROVIDERS = {"gpt-": "openai", "claude-": "anthropic", "gemini-": "vertex_ai"}


def get_llm_provider(model):
    """Return (provider, model_name) for a model string."""
    for prefix, provider in _PROVIDERS.items():
        if model.startswith(prefix):
            return provider, model
    return "openai", model


def token_counter(model="gpt-4o", text=""):
    """A rough token estimate: about four characters per token."""
    return max(1, len(text) // 4)
